WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.462
A paper spinner falls, turning as it
goes. Does a bigger one fall more slowly?

2
00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:14.697
Today you find out the way a scientist
does: one whole investigation.

3
00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:19.749
Every investigation starts with
a question you can actually test.

4
00:00:19.969 --> 00:00:24.195
Ours is this: does a spinner with
bigger wings fall more slowly?

5
00:00:24.415 --> 00:00:29.569
There are five types of scientific
enquiry. The question tells you which.

6
00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:34.898
Research. Fair testing. Observing over
time. Classifying. Pattern seeking.

7
00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:39.622
We change one thing and see what
happens. So ours is a fair test.

8
00:00:40.122 --> 00:00:43.373
A variable is anything in
the test that could change.

9
00:00:43.593 --> 00:00:47.401
You change one variable. Ours
is the size of the wings.

10
00:00:47.621 --> 00:00:51.569
You measure one variable. Ours
is how long it takes to fall.

11
00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:57.733
Every other variable stays the same: the height,
the paper, the paperclip, the person dropping.

12
00:00:57.953 --> 00:01:02.272
Change two things at once, and you cannot
tell which one made the difference.

13
00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:07.787
Choose your equipment before you
start. Each job needs the right tool.

14
00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:11.583
To time the fall, take a
stopwatch. It reads in seconds.

15
00:01:11.803 --> 00:01:16.308
To set the drop height, take a tape
measure. It reads in centimetres.

16
00:01:16.528 --> 00:01:22.287
Seconds and centimetres are standard units:
the same for everyone, so anybody can check.

17
00:01:22.787 --> 00:01:28.220
Predict first. Which spinner takes longer
to fall, the small one or the big one?

18
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.920
Drop it once and you get one number. Was
your thumb slow? Did a draught catch it?

19
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.852
One measurement can be wrong. So drop each
spinner three times, from the same height.

20
00:01:40.072 --> 00:01:44.345
The small spinner gave 2.1,
then 2.3, then 2.0 seconds.

21
00:01:44.565 --> 00:01:50.741
The spinner with bigger wings gave
2.8, then 3.0, then 2.7 seconds.

22
00:01:50.961 --> 00:01:58.066
Close, but not the same. Small differences
are normal. Three close numbers are reliable.

23
00:01:58.566 --> 00:02:02.421
Write each drop down as it
happens, in a table, in seconds.

24
00:02:02.641 --> 00:02:07.749
Drop one took 2.1 seconds, drop
two 2.3, and drop three 2.0.

25
00:02:07.969 --> 00:02:12.892
Now a dot plot: one dot for each
measurement, above its own time.

26
00:02:13.112 --> 00:02:19.196
The three dots pile up close to 2.1
seconds. An odd one would stand alone.

27
00:02:19.696 --> 00:02:25.500
The small spinner took about 2.1
seconds. The bigger wings took about 2.8.

28
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.411
Two clusters, and the bigger wings
took longer on every single drop.

29
00:02:30.631 --> 00:02:36.018
That is the pattern: bigger wings,
slower fall. It matches your prediction.

30
00:02:36.238 --> 00:02:41.207
So the conclusion answers the question you
asked, and the repeats make it reliable.

31
00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:46.119
A fair test changes one thing and
keeps every other thing the same.

32
00:02:46.339 --> 00:02:51.447
Choose the right kit, measure in standard
units, and repeat every measurement.

33
00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:56.683
Plot your drops as dots, and let the
cluster answer the question you asked.
